13
00:40:37,500 --> 00:40:43,500
Drive is a 2011 American action drama film directed by Nicolas Winding Refn.

14
00:43:45,000 --> 00:43:51,000
The screenplay, written by Hossein Amini, is based on James Sallis's 2005 novel.

15
00:46:52,500 --> 00:46:58,500
The film stars Ryan Gosling as an unnamed Hollywood stunt
driver who moonlights as a getaway driver.

16
00:50:00,000 --> 00:50:06,000
He quickly grows fond of his neighbor, Irene (Carey
Mulligan) and her young son, Benicio.

17
00:53:07,500 --> 00:53:13,500
When her debt-ridden husband, Standard (Oscar Isaac), is released from prison, the two men take part in what
turns out to be a failed million-dollar heist that endangers the lives of everyone involved.

18
00:56:15,000 --> 00:56:21,000
The film co-stars Bryan Cranston, Albert Brooks,
Christina Hendricks, and Ron Perlman.

19
00:59:22,500 --> 00:59:28,500
Producers Marc Platt and Adam Siegel optioned the source novel
after Siegel read a review from Publishers Weekly.

20
01:02:30,000 --> 01:02:36,000
Adapting the book proved to be challenging for
Amini, as it had a nonlinear narrative.

21
01:05:37,500 --> 01:05:43,500
Gosling, one of Platt's top casting choices, eventually signed on for the
lead, as he wanted to star in an action-oriented project.

22
01:08:45,000 --> 01:08:51,000
Gosling played a pivotal role in the film's production, which included
hiring Refn as director and Beth Mickle as production designer.

23
01:11:52,500 --> 01:11:58,500
Newton Thomas Sigel oversaw the principal photography, which started on September 25,
2010, was shot on location in Los Angeles, and ended on November 12.

24
01:15:00,000 --> 01:15:06,000
Before its September 2011 release, Drive had been shown at the 64th
Cannes Film Festival, earning Refn the Best Director Award.

25
01:18:07,500 --> 01:18:13,500
The film was praised for its direction, cinematography, performances (particularly Gosling and Brooks's), visuals, action sequences, and musical score;
however, some critics were appalled by its graphic violence and found it to be potentially detrimental to the film's box office success.

26
01:21:15,000 --> 01:21:21,000
Nonetheless, the film was still a commercial success, grossing over
$81 million against a production budget of $15 million.

27
01:24:22,500 --> 01:24:28,500
Several critics listed Drive as one of the best films of
2011, including the National Board of Review.

28
01:27:30,000 --> 01:27:36,000
Its accolades include a nomination for Best Sound
Editing at the 84th Academy Awards.

29
01:30:37,500 --> 01:30:43,500
The film has garnered a cult following since release.

30
01:33:45,000 --> 01:33:51,000
The film explores themes of robbery, car mechanic,
stuntman, beaten to death, revenge.

31
01:36:52,500 --> 01:36:58,500
The tone is intense and dark and emotional.
